         <description><![CDATA[<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/3396f23c-e65a-4139-aaaa-9470647e51ea,1795633607768/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C0%2C6000%2C3375%29%3B' alt='In the photo (left to right): Catherine Gentilcore and Marie-Claude Sarrazin. Photo taken at the National Assembly, Quebec City, Canada, November 25, 2026. Photo by Sylvain Roy Roussel, CBC/Radio-Canada.' width='620' height='349' title='Parti Québécois House Leader Catherine Gentilcore (left) and Quebec&apos;s Minister of Justice, Marie-Claude Sarrazin (right) confirmed at the National Assembly on Wednesday that their party was ready to sit as planned next Tuesday, regardless of the outcome of ongoing legal proceedings relating to legislators having to swear an oath to the king.'/><p>The battle over whether Quebec legislators will have to swear an oath to the King continues, with an appeal hearing scheduled for Friday. The premier wants his opponents to join him in sitting in the legislative session, regardless of the outcome of the court ruling.</p>]]></description>
