      <item>
         <title><![CDATA[El Niño is getting some monster-sized nicknames. But is the weather actually getting more extreme?]]></title>
         <link>https://www.cbc.ca/news/climate/super-mega-godzilla-el-nino-9.7433588?cmp=rss</link>
         <description><![CDATA[<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/12086259-64cf-46f3-a300-50fc36da9693,1795637758390/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C0%2C5447%2C3063%29%3B' alt='Big waves crashing in front of beach front homes.' width='620' height='349' title='A drone view shows waves being held back by barriers erected in front of beach front homes as a King Tide, El Nino warm waters and large surf impact the coastal area of Dana Point, California, US, November 25, 2026.'/><p>From "Super" to "Godzilla" to "Mega," the names for this year's powerful El Niño keep getting bigger. But scientists say there's no official upgrade. Here's what the dramatic labels mean, how exceptional this event really is and what Canadians can expect this winter.</p>]]></description>
         <pubDate>Wed, 25 Nov 2026 04:00:00 EST</pubDate>
         <category>News/Climate</category>
